In this lesson we'll look at how to recover a whole function when you only know its gradient and one extra clue. Here's the big idea: a derivative tells you the slope at every point, but there are actually lots of functions with that exact same slope, because you can slide the graph up or down without changing its steepness. That vertical shift is the mystery constant everyone calls C. So the recipe goes like this. First, you anti-differentiate the gradient function to get a family of curves, written as a rule plus C. That step is just integration in reverse. Next, you take the single piece of extra information, like the value of the function at a particular point, and you plug it in. That turns the family of curves into one specific equation, because it pins down exactly how far up or down the graph sits. Once you know C, you rewrite the rule with that number slotted in, and you're done. The same idea shows up in motion problems. If you know velocity, anti-differentiate once to get displacement, and you'll need an initial position to fix C. If you start from acceleration, you'll anti-differentiate twice, picking up two constants along the way, and use both the initial position and the initial velocity to lock them in. Now let's see it in action.